Change(genesis='e30427e699992327267add669c3bc989f6d16525ba6f493121eec7778638e0ce', deps=('df49a36ad21ef68eb9bfa50ee541beaa2ecfc145651affabe08cd1c1eee7e76a',), event=Authored(todo='directmailonboarding', at=datetime(2026, 11, 21, 22, 40, 56, 389623), actor='bmabsout', kind='todo', created=datetime(2026, 11, 21, 0, 0, 0), body='A new deployment connects an ordinary IMAP and SMTP account directly, with no mail tools to install first; the local mail index stays as an option', spec=Flat(value=0.9), rationale=('The first hour of a new deployment is spent on four mail tools before', 'the app shows anything.'), category='', waiting_on='', detail="A direction, noted on 2026-11-21. What the mail index gives the application today is four things: a full-text index over about sixty thousand messages, threads, the reading of a message's parts, and tags; the fetching is other tools. None of it is the library's kind of work: mail is a copy of what a server holds, written once and merged by nobody, and it is kept as a cache that can be rebuilt. So the unification is not mail as events. It is one source behind the mail port that fetches in process and keeps each message by the hash of its bytes, with one embedded full-text index that is derived from what is kept and can be rebuilt, the same index over the application's own stores (conversations, todos, memory), which have no search of their own today. The index is then a derived view as a fold is, and the external index stays as an optional backend behind the same port. To measure first: an embedded index against the present one on the same messages (build time, size, the queries the pages and the secretary ask), and what of threading and of the parts' reading the application would own.", source=None, subtodos=(), notes=(), note=''))